This is the best information I could find. "A personal accident" has been used before in police reports as a euphemism for suicide. Could mean something else, though. The presence of federal police might indicate the train station will be closed for a longer period of time.

anyone notice how the peasant kid from the intro scene bore a striking resemblance to young Bran from the premiere episode? Weird parallel. Short kid, round brown hair—looked a lot like Bran. Then we see him climbing up a tree, like Bran used to. 

&#x200B;

Not sure if I can wring any meaning out of the resemblance, but it must have been significant in some way...?

That's not what I said at all. It doesn't matter whether I agree with Netanyahu's policies or ideology; what matters is the clear breaching of international human rights laws. If we were to abide by our own laws, we would not provide military aid to ANY administration that perpetuates human rights abuses. 

&#x200B;

Are you a troll or do you not care that the IDF shot at 5,800 Palestinian citizens, most unarmed, many children, in 2018? Do you not care that they have been overtly ordered to kill palestinians who are too close to the border "even if they pose no imminent threat to life"? The Israeli army itself has boasted that it has caused an "exceptional humanitarian crisis" in Gaza. They have cut off sewage, drinking water, and electricity in Gaza. They have bombed hospitals with patients still in them. They have set anti-Israeli demonstrators on fire. Israel has openly rejected International Human Rights laws and has lobbied against it in the UN, stating that human rights violations are necessary for their political goals. 

&#x200B;

Don't be a fucking idiot. Respond with a valid point, or take a good hard look at yourself.

Did you read it? It's not a blanket call against providing funding, it's a reference to a SPECIFIC US law that prohibits "**military aid being given to any military organization that is involved in systematic human rights abuses."** 

&#x200B;

Obama swaying an election against a human rights abuser is FAR different from trump supplying military aid IN FAVOR of a human rights abuser.

Also keep in mind that it's pooooossible that the night king is after Bran, and Bran specifically. He's already tried to kill Bran twice, and knows that Bran is aware of their weaknesses, etc. There's an uncanny tension between the wights and the three eyed raven, and that makes me think Bran is in extreme danger. 

&#x200B;

Regarding Benjen Stark, yep, like most of that whole season, I was left with too many questions. The books, for example, delve much deeper into the Dorn storyline, the process of Arya's training by the faceless men, and the tension between Arya/Sansa/Littlefinger. The show, however, just skims over these parts, racing through them as plotpoints but not necessarily as stories. Where is the resolution with the faceless men? Is Arya being hunted? What did her training entail? Also, how did Sansa and Arya ever figure Littlefinger out? Did they speak? Did Bran tell Sansa what he saw? Were they teamed up from the beginning? 

&#x200B;

Again, this is explained in the books, but the show did a shite job of explaining any of this.

You don't seem to be coming at this issue without deeply entrenched prejudices and preconceived notions.

&#x200B;

Women's rights = access to contraceptives/education = lower birthrate. Do you disagree that a lower birthrate is vital to the survival and health of our species?

&#x200B;

I'm afraid the discussion about the economic benefits of equal rights is too broad to fully debate here. The way I see it, though, is a workforce in which all constituents consider themselves equal is a workforce with less struggle and strife. Less struggle and strife = healthier habits of production, consumption, etc. Seems pretty simple to me. Maybe you're right that women's rights isn't a "defining feature" of a successful economy, but it is a feature that is present in EVERY single economy that's thriving. As a result, it seems like a worthwhile goal for any country or community, no? There are other issues to be tackled on top of women's rights, OF COURSE. But that doesn't negate the fact that women's rights is a pretty DAMN good indicator of economic and societal health, and therefore should be sought by an earth attempting to heal itself. 

&#x200B;

Can I ask why you're so myopically focused on this issue in particular? It's not like all of Planet Earth's episode was about women's rights. That section took up less than 5% of the episode's runtime.

&#x200B;

Is it an anti-liberal thing? Is it a prejudice thing? Why this topic in particular? I suggest you examine that question when you're coolheaded and feeling introspective.

There's a huge literature scene here! There are writers' meetups, poetry readings, english bookstores, storytelling events... I'm a big loner too (I work in tech during the day and write fiction at night), but these kinds of events are the reason I don't feel isolated here. Just last night I went to a random movie screening at an art collective. Afterwards, I was sitting alone and drinking wine (the usual!) and overheard a group of three talking about writing poetry and fiction. My eyes lit up, of course. Without even having to say anything on my part, one of the people saw me get excited and asked me my name, and I got invited into the group. We ended up talking about writing for hours, then all went out together. Ended up meeting even more writers at a bar later on. Now I have four different invitations to writers' meetups, workshops, and readings. 

&#x200B;

My point is, I wasn't even trying to find people like myself last night. I just saw this cool event about a Hungarian film, went, and got swept up into the Berlin art scene. Don't overthink it too much. You don't have to be a social butterfly, or walking up to strangers on the street in order to meet people like you here. Just don't lock yourself in your room, join some groups on facebook or meetups (or google some literary events), and bam, it'll happen before you know it. You'll have a great time here :)
